\fs28 \cf2 \cb5 The manifest includes the OBS overlay and event log, so those are actual hashed artifacts rather than just decorative telemetry.\cf0 \cb1 \
\cf2 \cb5 An important distinction: it verifies the daily Git snapshot, 
\f2\b not the underlying bytes of videos stored on YouTube
\f0\b0 . Remote media references remain unverified unless you separately obtain and hash those files. Cryptographic integrity also isn't proof of authorship or recording time without an external trust anchor.\cf0 \cb1 \
\pard\pardeftab720\partightenfactor0

\f4\fs24 \cf2 \cb5 <$Scr_H::2>
\f5\b\fs48 The architectural decision I particularly like\cf0 \cb1 \
\pard\pardeftab720\partightenfactor0

\f4\b0\fs24 \cf2 \cb5 <!$Scr_H::2>
\f0\fs28 The daily repo isn't the video repository.\cf0 \cb1 \
\cf2 \cb5 It's the 
\f2\b coordination and provenance record
\f0\b0  for everything that happened that day.\cf0 \cb1 \
\cf2 \cb5 The bulky multimodal data can live wherever it makes most sense. The daily repo contains the small, highly structured information that helps reconstruct what those data represent.\cf0 \cb1 \
\cf2 \cb5 That's a useful foundation for later retrieval, evaluation, and potentially multimodal training-data preparation.\cf0 \cb1 \
\cf2 \cb5 I deliberately didn't build OBS WebSocket automation, cloud API uploads, video/frame hashing, or AI-model instrumentation yet. Those are natural next layers, but none is required to begin collecting coherent daily records. The GitHub integration is implemented using 
\f7\fs26 \cb7 gh
\f0\fs28 \cb5 , but live remote publishing wasn't tested in this environment.\cf0 \cb1 \
\cf2 \cb5 My suggested next step would simply be to install the tool and run it during one actual work session. No elaborate setup, no production spectacle\'97just see whether switching modes and noting events feels pleasantly trivial or annoyingly intrusive.\cf0 \cb1 \
\cf2 \cb5 If it feels trivial, that's a good foundation for a more serious Rust implementation later.\cf0 \cb1 \
\pard\pardeftab720\partightenfactor0

